      "hints": [
        "Set discriminant $\\Delta = (3k)^2 - 4(2)(k) > 0 \\implies 9k^2 - 8k > 0$.",
        "Factorise $k(9k - 8) > 0$ to get $k < 0$ or $k > \\frac{8}{9}$."
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "For the quadratic equation $2x^2 + 3kx + k = 0$ to have two distinct real roots, its discriminant must be strictly positive:\n$$\\Delta = b^2 - 4ac > 0$$\n$$(3k)^2 - 4(2)(k) > 0 \\implies 9k^2 - 8k > 0$$",
        "Factorise the quadratic inequality:\n$$k(9k - 8) > 0$$",
        "State the outside solution intervals:\n$$k < 0 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad k > \\frac{8}{9} \\quad \\left(\\text{or } (-\\infty, 0) \\cup \\left(\\frac{8}{9}, \\infty\\right)\\right)$$"
      ],
